{"abstract":[{"text":"How long a rest must be to split a part into separate sequences.","type":"text"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"enum","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"RestSplit","kind":"identifier"}],"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"kind":"mentions","mentions":["doc:\/\/IvorModel\/documentation\/IvorModel\/UsingIvorModel"]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A long rest is usually a break between phrases, or bars where the part is silent, rather"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"than part of a phrase. Splitting there ends one sequence at the note before the rest and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"starts another at the note after it, as if they were separate parts: the template learns"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"each phrase’s ending as an ending and its opening as an opening, and never learns the rest"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"itself. Shorter rests are learned as usual."}]}]}],"topicSections":[{"anchor":"Enumeration-Cases","generated":true,"title":"Enumeration Cases","identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorModel\/documentation\/IvorModel\/Template\/AnalysisOptions-swift.struct\/RestSplit-swift.enum\/beat(_:)","doc:\/\/IvorModel\/documentation\/IvorModel\/Template\/AnalysisOptions-swift.struct\/RestSplit-swift.enum\/wall(_:)"]},{"anchor":"Default-Implementations","generated":true,"title":"Default Implementations","identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorModel\/documentation\/IvorModel\/Template\/AnalysisOptions-swift.struct\/RestSplit-swift.enum\/Decodable-Implementations","doc:\/\/IvorModel\/documentation\/IvorModel\/Template\/AnalysisOptions-swift.struct\/RestSplit-swift.enum\/Equatable-Implementations"]}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"patch":0,"minor":3},"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"